<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alamgir II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1699">1699</a>–<a href="/years/1759">1759</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alamgir II was Mughal emperor from 1754 to 1759, a son of Jahandar Shah placed on the throne by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi-ud-din-khan-imad-ul-mulk">Ghazi-ud-din Khan Imad-ul-Mulk</a>. He held little real power, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1757 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and the vizier competed for control. Fearing that the emperor was conspiring against him, Imad-ul-Mulk had him murdered in 1759.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alamgir-ii.jpg" alt="Alamgir II" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sukha Luhar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alamgir_II_of_India.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mirza Aziz-ud-Din Muhammad was born on 6 June 1699 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burhanpur">Burhanpur</a>, the second son of Muizz-ud-Din, who as Jahandar Shah later became emperor, and a grandson of the emperor Bahadur Shah I. His mother was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> princess, Anup Bai. He was seven when his great-grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. After Jahandar Shah was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukhsiyar">Farrukhsiyar</a>, Aziz-ud-Din was imprisoned in 1714 and remained in captivity until 1754, so he had no experience of administration or warfare.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The vizier Imad-ul-Mulk, who had deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-bahadur">Ahmad Shah Bahadur</a> in 1754, released him and on 2 June 1754 gave him the title Alamgir II, hoping to follow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>'s centralised approach. He was 55, regarded as frail, and a weak ruler, with all power in the vizier's hands. Imad-ul-Mulk hired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> mercenaries, put the imperial revenues into his own pocket and starved the emperor's family. The empire nevertheless began to re-centralise, as many nawabs sought the emperor's help in resisting the Marathas, a development Imad-ul-Mulk did not welcome.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> again and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. In January 1757 Alamgir II and his courtiers went to meet him, and ties were strengthened when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a>'s son Timur Shah was chosen as the suitor of the emperor's daughter Zuhra Begum. In July 1757 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> under Raghunathrao besieged Delhi. Najib-ul-Daula, the emperor's Mir Bakhshi, resisted for more than five months before withdrawing to Najibabad, and the Marathas looted the city. The emperor and his family fled to Bharatpur and returned with the help of a Jat regiment sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suraj-mal">Suraj Mal</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, Alamgir II refused to recognise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a> as nawab after Plassey in 1757.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Imad-ul-Mulk and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadashivrao-bhau">Sadashivrao Bhau</a> came to believe that the emperor was about to advance his son Ali Gauhar, who had escaped from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in the summer of 1759, and they plotted his murder. Alamgir II was assassinated in November 1759, on 29 November, and Shah Jahan III was placed on the throne. His son-in-law Timur Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a>'s defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> in 1760 provoked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> to return, and Delhi was recaptured and placed under the nominal authority of Shah Alam II before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat">Third Battle of Panipat</a> in 1761.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alamgir II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ghazi-ud-din Khan Imad-ul-Mulk, the Mughal vizier who had deposed Ahmad Shah Bahadur and murdered <b>Alamgir II</b>, later took refuge with the Marathas.</blockquote><a href="/state/baoni-state">Baoni State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.2,71.47&amp;year=1738" aria-label="Multan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Alamgir II"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5842 4941h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5842 4941h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.2,71.47&amp;year=1738">What was here in 1738? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
